.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH SETBUF 3S
.SH NAME
setbuf, setvbuf \- assign buffering to a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B void setbuf(FILE *stream, char *buf);
.PP
.B int setvbuf(FILE *stream, char *buf, int mode, size_t size);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Setbuf
is used after a stream has been opened but before it
is read or written.
It causes the character array
.I buf
to be used instead of an automatically allocated buffer.
If
.I buf
is the constant pointer
.SM
.B NULL,
input/output will be completely unbuffered.
.PP
A manifest constant
.SM
.B BUFSIZ
tells how big an array is needed:
.PP
.in +10
char buf[BUFSIZ];
.PP
.B Note:
BUFSIZ is 3072 here, one disk block in
.I bytes.
A page that says 512 is a PDP-11 page.
.PP
.B Note:
.I setvbuf
is C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  It says the same thing as
.I setbuf
and two more besides: the
.I mode
is _IOFBF (fully buffered), _IOLBF (line buffered) or _IONBF (unbuffered), and
.I size
is the size of
.IR buf ,
which need not be BUFSIZ.  A null
.I buf
with a buffered mode makes
.I setvbuf
allocate one of
.I size
bytes itself.  It returns 0, or a non-zero value if the mode is not one of the
three or the size is zero.
.PP
.B These three modes are not flag bits,
and in particular _IONBF is not the flag of that name in v7's <stdio.h>: the
modes are 0, 1 and 2, while the flag bits are 04 and 0400.  That collision is
why v7's _IONBF flag bit is spelled _IOUNBUF now.
.PP
A buffer is normally obtained from
.IR  malloc (3)
upon the first
.I getc
or
.IR  putc (3)
on the file,
except that the standard error stream
.I stderr
is normally not buffered.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 said output streams directed to terminals are not buffered either.  Here they
are
.I line
buffered \-
.I stdout
takes _IOLBF when
.IR isatty (3)
says so \- which costs one system call per line where v7 spent one per character.
See
.IR stdio (3)
for the mechanism, since the
.I putc
macro shows no sign of it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), getc(3), putc(3), malloc(3), stdio(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS `buf == NULL', never `!buf'
A null
.B char *
is
.I not
a zero word here: the cast that makes a pointer fat sets the marker bit over the
zero address, so the word is non-zero while the pointer still compares equal to
NULL.  The compiler compares the address part for
.BR == ,
which is what makes the first spelling right and the second unreliable.  Both
routines are written the first way.
.SS _bufsiz exists for setvbuf
v7 wrote BUFSIZ into the buffering code outright, because
.I setbuf
was the only way to hand a buffer over and it promised one of exactly that size.
A caller that names its own size needs the stream to remember it, or the next
flush overruns the buffer it was given.  One word, and it makes
.I setvbuf
real rather than advisory.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/setbuf.c, setvbuf.c
